Chapter: EmojiStillsSpec
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter EmojiStills
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 EmojiStills: eight baked animal tables, selected by Unicode codepoint.

 THE DISPATCH IS BY CODEPOINT AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES IT WORTH GRADING. Where
 CatStills selects a pose by a small index, this chapter selects an animal by
 numbers like 129414 and 128024 -- six digits apart in ways no reader checks by
 eye, and adjacent in ways that matter: giraffe is 129426 and zebra is 129427.
 A transposed digit or an off-by-one lands on a real animal and draws the wrong
 one, silently, forever. All eight are graded and so is the miss.

 THE BULL IS THE ONLY ANIMAL WITH GRADIENTS AND THAT IS ASSERTED, not observed.
 Forty of its forty-three polygons carry a StillGrad; every other table in this
 port is solid. The chapter's own prose says the bull has a stop at alpha zero --
 a gradient fading to nothing rather than to a colour -- so it is the single
 exerciser of the shaded path in the whole renderer. If a regenerated table lost
 those gradients the bull would still draw, as a flat shape, and nothing else
 would complain.

 THE VALUES ARE CAPTURED AND SAY SO, as in CatStillsSpec: bake_stills.py
 generates this from the art and the oracle for the drawing is the blitter diff.
 What this file asks is whether the chapter compiles, loads, dispatches, and
 still holds what it held.

 We say:

Section: Counting

  pts-in : List StillPoly, Integer -> Integer
  pts-in (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0
    else let p = list-at ps i in list-length p.pts + pts-in ps (i + 1)

  grads-in : List StillPoly, Integer -> Integer
  grads-in (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0
    else let p = list-at ps i in list-length p.grad + grads-in ps (i + 1)

Section: The Eight Tables

 In chapter order: duck, elephant, giraffe, zebra, rhino, bull, cow, pig.

  poly-got : List Integer
  poly-got = [list-length duck-polys, list-length elephant-polys, list-length giraffe-polys, list-length zebra-polys, list-length rhino-polys, list-length bull-polys, list-length cow-polys, list-length pig-polys]

  poly-want : List Integer
  poly-want = [6, 6, 24, 22, 6, 43, 16, 6]

  pts-got : List Integer
  pts-got = [pts-in duck-polys 0, pts-in elephant-polys 0, pts-in giraffe-polys 0, pts-in zebra-polys 0, pts-in rhino-polys 0, pts-in bull-polys 0, pts-in cow-polys 0, pts-in pig-polys 0]

  pts-want : List Integer
  pts-want = [189, 257, 850, 713, 237, 2309, 469, 243]

 Forty gradients, all of them the bull's.

  grad-got : List Integer
  grad-got = [grads-in duck-polys 0, grads-in elephant-polys 0, grads-in giraffe-polys 0, grads-in zebra-polys 0, grads-in rhino-polys 0, grads-in bull-polys 0, grads-in cow-polys 0, grads-in pig-polys 0]

  grad-want : List Integer
  grad-want = [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 40, 0, 0]

Section: The Dispatch

 Eight codepoints and two misses. 129425 sits between giraffe at 129426 and
 rhino at 129423 and belongs to neither, which is the miss a fat-fingered
 codepoint would actually produce; 0 is the trivial one.

  disp-got : List Integer
  disp-got = [list-length (emoji-polys-for 129414), list-length (emoji-polys-for 128024), list-length (emoji-polys-for 129426), list-length (emoji-polys-for 129427), list-length (emoji-polys-for 129423), list-length (emoji-polys-for 128002), list-length (emoji-polys-for 128004), list-length (emoji-polys-for 128022), list-length (emoji-polys-for 129425), list-length (emoji-polys-for 0)]

  disp-want : List Integer
  disp-want = [6, 6, 24, 22, 6, 43, 16, 6, 0, 0]

 GIRAFFE AND ZEBRA ARE ADJACENT CODEPOINTS WITH DIFFERENT TABLE SIZES, so the
 line above already separates them -- 24 polygons against 22. Duck and elephant
 are not: both have 6, as do rhino and pig. Those four are told apart by their
 first colour, which is the cheapest thing that distinguishes them.

  col-got : List Integer
  col-got = [(list-at (emoji-polys-for 129414) 0).color, (list-at (emoji-polys-for 128024) 0).color, (list-at (emoji-polys-for 129423) 0).color, (list-at (emoji-polys-for 128022) 0).color, (list-at (emoji-polys-for 128002) 0).color, (list-at (emoji-polys-for 128004) 0).color]

  col-want : List Integer
  col-want = [4493595, 6513507, 13882323, 16735833, 4989733, 10197915]

Section: The Shaded Path

 The bull's first gradient, read through the at-most-one list: its kind, its two
 stops, and the offsets. Kind 1 is linear, 2 is radial. The stops are 0xAARRGGBB
 rather than the 0xRRGGBB a solid polygon carries, because these composite.

  bull-grad : StillGrad
  bull-grad = list-at (list-at bull-polys 0).grad 0

  bg-int-got : List Integer
  bg-int-got = [list-length (list-at bull-polys 0).grad, bull-grad.kind]

  bg-int-want : List Integer
  bg-int-want = [1, 1]

  bg-real-got : List Real
  bg-real-got = [bull-grad.off0, bull-grad.off1]

  bg-real-want : List Real
  bg-real-want = [0.0, 1.0]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "es-poly" poly-got poly-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "es-pts " pts-got pts-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "es-grad" grad-got grad-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "es-disp" disp-got disp-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "es-col " col-got col-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "es-bgi " bg-int-got bg-int-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "es-bgr " bg-real-got bg-real-want 0.0)
  end
